WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.620
Look along the spinal cord of a chick embryo.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.020
The sensory clusters called dorsal root ganglia sit in a neat, repeating row.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.320
No central planner lays them out.

4
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.980
Their cells come from the neural crest, a population at the top of the neural tube.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.000
So what turns a crowd of wandering cells into a row?

6
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:24.853
Crest cells begin as part of the tube's top edge.

7
00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:27.113
They loosen their grip on their neighbours.

8
00:00:27.573 --> 00:00:28.733
Then they crawl away.

9
00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:34.773
That change, from a cell in a sheet to a free cell, is called an epithelial to

10
00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:36.353
mesenchymal transition.

11
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.353
Biologists shorten it to EMT.

12
00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:45.377
In the chick trunk, one route, called ventromedial, runs through the somites.

13
00:00:46.277 --> 00:00:48.857
Somites are the blocks of tissue beside the tube.

14
00:00:49.377 --> 00:00:53.057
The part of each block that will form vertebrae is the sclerotome.

15
00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:58.317
It has a front half, called rostral, and a back half, called caudal.

16
00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:01.597
Crest cells pass only through the rostral half.

17
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.500
The caudal half keeps them out, using several cues.

18
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.960
Crest cells carry a receptor called FB.

19
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.060
Cells of the caudal half carry a signal on their surface, called ephrin-B.

20
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:18.360
Because it sits in the membrane, it works only when cells touch.

21
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.800
When a crest cell touches ephrin-B, the signal into the crest cell repels it, and it

22
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:24.800
is pushed away.

23
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:27.323
Researchers tested this in a dish.

24
00:01:28.043 --> 00:01:33.183
They laid alternating stripes of Efren B and a control protein, and plated trunk

25
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:34.463
crest cells on them.

26
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:37.483
The cells avoided the Efren B stripes.

27
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:41.003
A sceptic could say the stripe is just a poor surface.

28
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:46.383
So they added soluble Efren B to the medium, meaning free in the liquid.

29
00:01:46.743 --> 00:01:47.943
That blocked the avoidance.

30
00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:50.903
The avoidance needs F and Efren signaling.

31
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:54.597
Back in the embryo, the same rule applies.

32
00:01:55.537 --> 00:02:00.417
Crest cells enter the rostral half of each sclerotome and not the caudal half.

33
00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:05.197
That is thought to help explain why the dorsal root ganglia are segmented.

34
00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:08.057
Cells are not only drawn toward their targets.

35
00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:11.357
Where crest cells may not go helps shape what they build.
